as, egocentric, not willing to further education. So C strong on reliability, attention, decision quality, but communication and adaptability concerns.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility (takes responsibility seriously), concentration (can concentrate very well), problem solving (solves problems extremely well), responds to unexpected events adequately (stress?), but concerns: arrogant, loner (crew cooperation). So D strong on responsibility, attention, problem solving, but crew cooperation concerns.\n\nCandidate A: technical competence (anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, spatial vision), leadership, but concerns: not good at taking criticism (crew cooperation), eats unhealthily (maybe health reliability). No explicit reliability fact.\n\nThus, B and C both have strong reliability. C has decision quality and attention, B has stress handling and technical weather. Which is more critical? Decision quality and attention are crucial. Communication is also crucial. Both B and C have communication concerns. B's tone, C's difficulty communicating ideas. Which is worse? Possibly difficulty communicating ideas is more severe for cockpit. B's tone may be mitigated.\n\nThus maybe Candidate B still best. But we could argue that Candidate C's 100% reliability and excellent attention outweigh B's concerns. However, C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nWe could ask other panel members for more info on Candidate C's stress resilience or crew cooperation. But we have no tool to ask about that. We could ask Agent 1 for decision quality of B, but they already gave some. Actually Agent 1 asked for decision quality of B. We could ask them.\n\nBut we need to produce a public message now. We can either agree with B, or propose C as alternative. Let's propose that Candidate C's reliability and attention are strong, but communication concerns may be mitigated. Provide reasoning.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 1's point. So we ca